# Checkout stability of the fixtures.
#
# Git decides whether a file is text by looking for NUL bytes: a file with
# none is text, and with core.autocrlf turned on its LF line endings are
# rewritten to CRLF on checkout.  That rewrite does not show up in a diff,
# and it changes bytes the fixtures assert on:
#
#   * the PDF fixtures carry a classic cross-reference table of absolute
#     byte offsets, so a converted file points its offsets at the wrong
#     bytes and loses the document information;
#   * the compatibility corpus carries a sha256 per fixture in
#     MANIFEST.json, so a converted file no longer matches its digest.
#
# The rules below pin the fixtures to the bytes they are written with.

# Binary formats: never EOL-converted, never merged as text.
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.doc binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary

# The shared TSV corpora are read line by line, and the line endings are
# part of what they are:
#
#   * word_whitespace_corpus.tsv is written with newline="\n" by
#     backend/scripts/generate_word_shared_corpus.py, so LF is its canonical
#     form.  The CRLF it pins is an escape sequence inside a record, not a
#     line ending of the file.
#   * ip_policy_reference.tsv is written with CRLF line endings, so CRLF is
#     its canonical form.
#
# LF is the repository's canonical text form, so it is the default; the one
# CRLF corpus is named explicitly.
*.tsv text eol=lf
kotlin/ingest/src/test/resources/fixtures/ip_policy_reference.tsv text eol=crlf

# The compatibility corpus is digest-pinned by MANIFEST.json and written
# byte for byte by generate_corpus.py, so no file under it may change a
# byte on checkout.  This rule comes last because it is the last word:
# nothing under the corpus is converted, whatever the rules above say.
# It covers renamed.txt too, which is a Word package under a .txt name --
# a rule that made every .txt text would corrupt it.
backend/tests/fixtures/compatibility/** -text
